            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Cleisthenes</strong><br>\nCleisthenes was an Athenian leader known for reforming the constitution of ancient Athens and setting it on a democratic path. Born into the Alcmaeonid family, he was in a position of influence and power. In 508 B.C., Cleisthenes began to reorganize the political structure of Athens by breaking up the power of traditional tribal divisions. He introduced new geographical divisions known as demes, which served as the foundation of Athenian political organization.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cleisthenes' Reforms</strong><br>\nCleisthenes' reforms are often considered the birth of Athenian democracy. He established a system where power was distributed more equally among the citizens of Athens. His reforms included the creation of the Council of Five Hundred, which was responsible for proposing laws and decisions to the Assembly, and the institutions of ostracism and lot casting for public office positions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Council of Five Hundred</strong><br>\nThe Council of Five Hundred was a revolutionary body formed by Cleisthenes that represented the Athenian citizens. It was composed of 50 members from each of the ten Athenian tribes, selected annually by lot. The Council’s main role was to decide on matters that would be discussed in the Assembly, making the legislative process more orderly and inclusive.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
