            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Ostracism in Athenian Democracy</strong><br>\nOstracism was a procedure under the Athenian democracy in which a citizen could be expelled from the city-state of Athens for ten years. While this could be seen as harsh, it was used as a tool to protect the state from potential tyrants or powerful individuals whose ambitions might threaten the democracy. Cleisthenes introduced ostracism to prevent the rise of another tyrant like Hippias, whom he helped to overthrow.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cleisthenes and the Tribes</strong><br>\nCleisthenes redefined the structure of Athenian tribes by creating ten new tribes, each made up of several demes, or neighborhoods, from different regions of Attica. This meant that tribes were no longer based on family relations but on residence. This mix encouraged unity and reduced the influence of noble families in Athenian politics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Introduction of Lot Casting</strong><br>\nCleisthenes introduced the practice of choosing most officials by lot, a method that was considered more democratic than elections. This was because elections could be easily dominated by the wealthy and well-connected. Lot casting helped to ensure that all citizens had an equal chance of participating in the government, regardless of their social status.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
