          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Impact on Athenian Military</strong><br>\nThe reorganization of the tribes had a significant impact on the military structure of Athens as well. Each tribe provided a regiment to the Athenian army, which led to a more integrated and cooperative defense force. The military thus became a reflection of Cleisthenes' democratic ideals, as it was composed of citizen soldiers from all walks of Athenian life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Assembly of Citizens</strong><br>\nThe Assembly, or Ecclesia, was the principal body of Athenian democracy where citizens could directly vote on legislation and executive bills. Cleisthenes expanded the role of the Assembly, allowing it to become a forum where all male citizens could express their opinions and exercise their vote, highlighting the shift from aristocratic to democratic governance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Cleisthenes</strong><br>\nThe legacy of Cleisthenes lies in his transformation of Athens into a state where power was based on citizenship rather than heritage. His reforms laid the groundwork for the development of Athenian democracy and influenced political systems in subsequent centuries. Despite challenges, his vision of governance continues to be celebrated as a pivotal moment in the history of democracy.</p>\n"
              },
